course: dictionary
lesson: 14953
title: Words with more than one meaning
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. The word relations is one of them. To find the right meaning, read the words around it. «All our relations came for Christmas.» Here relations means your family: aunts, uncles and cousins.
explain: «The two countries wanted better relations after the argument.» Here relations means the way countries deal with each other. The clues are countries, argument and better. Try your meaning in the sentence: does "the way they get along" fit? Yes, so that is the right meaning.
explain: In maths, relations connect numbers, such as greater than or equal to. Ask yourself who or what the sentence is about: family, countries, or numbers? The topic tells you which meaning you need.
fact: Relations is a plural noun. One family member is a relation, and several are relations.
fact: «Relations» can mean family members, such as aunts, uncles and cousins. Another word for them is relatives.
fact: «Relations» can also mean the way people, groups or countries deal with each other, as in «good relations between neighbours».
fact: In formal writing, relations can mean connections between things, such as the relations between numbers.
fact: Useful phrases: friends and relations, public relations, foreign relations.
exercise: «All our relations came for Christmas.» Who are the relations in this sentence? | Rules in a game | Numbers on a page | Shops and roads in the town | *Aunts, uncles and cousins
exercise: «Good relations between the two neighbours made the street a happy place.» What does relations mean here? | *The way the neighbours get on with each other | Members of a family | Old stone houses | Numbers that are greater than ten
exercise: Which phrase is about how countries deal with each other? | Equal to | *Foreign relations | Family tree | Friends and relations
test: «The two countries have tense relations after the argument.» What does tense suggest about the relations? | *Not friendly, with a lot of strain | Very happy and relaxed | Long and thin | Made of soft cloth
test: In maths, relations show how numbers connect. Which of these is a relation? | Rivers | *Greater than | Cakes | Shapes
exam: «Ella's relations came to visit, so the house was full of cousins and aunts.» What does relations mean here? | Connections between numbers in a sum | *Family members, such as aunts and cousins | Rules for visiting a house | The way Ella gets along with her teacher
